WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.510
Heat, pressure, differential stress, and chemically active fluids can change rock

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:11.330
minerals or texture. Burial, tectonic activity, nearby magma, and fluid circulation

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.670
provide different combinations of those drivers.

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:17.830
Metamorphism and deformation may occur together or independently.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.530
Recrystallization can enlarge existing mineral crystals.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:27.650
Neocrystallization forms new minerals as reactions make a different assemblage stable.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.970
These terms describe different changes;

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.970
neither means the whole rock must melt and freeze again.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.430
Material can dissolve at stressed grain contacts and move in fluids.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.870
Chemical components can be redistributed, producing different mineral-rich layers.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:45.570
Whether the bulk composition changes depends on movement into or out

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.910
of the rock, which must be supported by evidence.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:54.690
During deformation, minerals and objects can rotate, flatten, stretch, or form

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:59.510
aligned fabrics. Metamorphism can preserve older features as relicts while adding

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.970
new minerals and foliation. Read the sequence of overprinting rather than

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.450
assuming all features formed simultaneously.
